== Particular Callings and the Wife’s Submission

=== §. 7. #emph[Of performing the duties of particular callings.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Eph 5:22.]

    #text(style: "italic")[Wives, submit yourselves#super[+] to your own husbands as to the Lord.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[From] that general direction about mutual submission, the apostle comes to certain particulars, by which he gives examples of it. And he teaches us that

#emph[It is not enough to perform the general duties of Christianity,] #footnote[Doctrine.] #emph[unless we are also conscientious in performing the particular duties of our separate callings.] A conscientious performance of those particular duties is one part of our walking “worthy of the calling” we have received.#footnote[Eph 4:1.] Therefore the apostle, to illustrate this and give examples of it, lists various particulars, both in this and #footnote[Col 3:18, #emph[etc.] 1 Cor 7. 1 Tim 3.] other epistles. So do #footnote[1 Pet 2. #emph[&] 3.] other apostles. And Titus#footnote[Titus 2.] is charged to teach them. God himself has given a pattern of this in his law: for the main aim of the fifth commandment #footnote[See more of this point in #emph[The whole armour of God,] Treatise 2, part 1, §. 4.] is to instruct us in the particular duties of our separate callings.

By this much credit is brought to our profession of faith, and “the teaching about God our Savior” is adorned.#footnote[Titus 2:10. 1 Pet 3:1, 2.] And by this much good is both shared with one another and received from one another. For our particular places and callings are the bonds by which persons are firmly and fitly knit together, as the members of a natural body are knit by nerves, arteries, sinews, veins and the like, by which life, sense and motion are passed from one to another.

Therefore let notice be taken of the particular callings #footnote[Use.] in which God has set us, and of the separate duties of those callings, and let conscience be used in practicing them. He is no good Christian who is careless in this. A bad husband, wife, parent, child, master, servant, magistrate or minister is no good Christian.

=== §. 8. #emph[Of the lawfulness of private functions in a family.]

Among other particular callings, the apostle chooses those which God has settled in private families. He is exact in reciting their separate and distinct orders (for a family consists of these three orders, #emph[husbands, wives, parents, children, masters, servants,] all of which he lists). Yes, he is also full and earnest in urging the duties which belong to them. From this we may well infer that

#emph[The private vocations of a family, and the functions belonging] #footnote[2 #emph[Doctrine.] Private duties of the family well become any Christian. οὐθὲν μάτην ἡ φύσις ποιεῖ. #emph[Arist. Polit. lib.] 1] #emph[to it, are such as Christians are called to by God, and in exercising them they may and must employ some part of their time.] For can we think that the Holy Spirit (who, as the philosophers say of nature, #emph[does nothing in vain)] would set down these private duties so distinctly, and urge them so forcibly, if they did not well become Christians and closely concern them? All the places in Scripture which require family duties are proofs of the truth of this doctrine.

The reasons for this doctrine are clear. For the family is #footnote[#emph[Reason.] The family a seedbed.] a seedbed of the church and commonwealth. It is like a beehive, in which is the stock, and out of which many swarms of bees are sent. For in families all sorts of people are bred and brought up, and out of families they are sent into the church and commonwealth. The first beginning of mankind, and of its increase, was out of a family. For God first joined Adam and Eve in marriage and made them husband and wife, and then gave them children. So husband and wife, parent and child (which are parts of a family) existed before magistrate and subject, minister and people, which are the parts of a commonwealth and a church. When all public societies were destroyed by the universal flood, a family, the family of Noah, was preserved, and out of it kingdoms and nations were raised again. That great people of the Jews, which could not be numbered for multitude, was raised out of the family of Abram. Yes, even to this day all sorts of people have come from families, and so they will to the end of the world. From this it follows that a conscientious performance of domestic and household duties tends to the good ordering of church and commonwealth, as being a means to fit and prepare men for them.

Besides, a family is a little church and a little commonwealth, #footnote[2 #emph[Reason.] A family is a representation of church and commonwealth.] or at least a lively representation of them. By it a trial may be made of those who are fit for any place of authority, or of subjection, in church or commonwealth. Or rather, it is like a school in which the first principles and grounds of government and subjection are learned, so that men are fitted for greater matters in church or commonwealth. That is why the #footnote[1 Tim 3:5.] apostle declares that a bishop who cannot rule his own house is not fit to govern the church. So we may say of inferiors who cannot be subject in a family: they will hardly be brought to yield such subjection as they ought in church or commonwealth. Take as an instance Absalom and Adonijah, David’s sons.

This is to be noted for the satisfaction of certain weak #footnote[1 #emph[Use.] Private callings in a family are sufficient callings.] consciences, who think that if they have no public calling, they have no calling at all, and from this conclude that all their time is spent without a calling. If that conclusion were good and sound, what comfort in spending their time would most women have, who are not admitted to any public function in church or commonwealth? Or servants, children and others who are wholly employed in the private affairs of the family? But the doctrine named above shows how unsound that conclusion is. Besides, who does not know that the preservation of families tends to the good of church and commonwealth? So a conscientious performance of household duties, in regard to its end and fruit, may be counted a public work. Yes, if domestic duties are well and thoroughly performed, they will be quite enough to take up a man’s whole time. If a master of a family is also a husband of a wife and a father of children, he will find work enough. This may easily be proved by those particular duties which we will show later to belong to masters, husbands and parents. So a wife likewise, if she is also a mother and a mistress, and faithfully endeavors to do what she is bound to do by virtue of those callings, will find enough to do. As for children under the government of their parents, and servants in a family, their whole calling is to be obedient to their parents and masters, and to do what they command them in the Lord. Therefore, if those who have no public calling are all the more diligent in the functions of their private callings, they will be as well accepted by the Lord as if they had public offices.

Yet there are many who, having no public employment, think they may spend their time as they please, either in idleness #footnote[2 #emph[Use.] Reproof of those who, having no public office, think themselves freed from all duty.] or in following their vain pleasures and delights day after day, and so cast themselves out of all calling. Such are many masters of families, who commit all the care of their house either to their wives or to some servant, and misspend their whole time in idleness, dissipation and sensual pleasure. Such are many mistresses, who spend their time lying in bed, dressing themselves and gossiping. Such are many young gentlemen living in their fathers’ houses, who, partly through the excessive indulgence and negligence of their parents, and partly through their own headstrong desires and rebellious will, run without restraint wherever their corrupt lusts lead them. These, and others like them, though by God’s providence they are placed in callings, in lawful callings, and in callings that give them matter enough of employment, yet make themselves to be of no calling. Now what blessing can they look for from the Lord? The Lord is accustomed #footnote[God’s blessing on men diligent in their calling.] to give his blessing to men while they are busy in their callings. Jacob’s#footnote[Gen 31:42.] faithful service to his uncle Laban moved God to bless him. Joseph’s#footnote[Gen 39:2.] faithfulness to his master Potiphar was remembered by God, who advanced him to be ruler in Egypt. Moses#footnote[Exod 3:1, 2.] was keeping his father-in-law’s sheep when God appeared to him in the bush and appointed him a prince over his people. David#footnote[1 Sam 16:11.] was sent for from the field, where he was keeping his father’s sheep, when he was anointed to be king over Israel. Elisha#footnote[1 Kgs 19:19.] was plowing when he was anointed to be a prophet. #footnote[Luke 2:8.] The shepherds were watching their sheep when the glad tidings were brought to them that the Savior of the world was born. Not to dwell on any more particulars, the promise of God’s protection is limited to our callings: for #footnote[Ps 91:11.] the charge which God has given to the angels concerning man is to guard him #emph[in all his ways.]

As for those who have public offices in church or #footnote[3\. #emph[Use.] Public callings must not hinder private duties.] commonwealth, they must not on that account think themselves exempt from all family duties. These private duties are necessary duties. Though a man is a magistrate or a minister, yet if he is a husband, or a father, or a master, he must not neglect his wife, children and servants. Indeed, those who are free from public functions are bound to attend all the more to the private duties of their families, because they have more leisure for them. But no one ought wholly to neglect them. Joshua,#footnote[Josh 24:15.] who was a captain and prince of his people, and very much employed in public affairs, yet did not neglect his family: for he professes that he and #emph[his house] would serve the Lord. It seems that Eli#footnote[1 Sam 2:29; 3:13.] was negligent in performing the duty of a father, and David#footnote[1 Kgs 1:6.] also. But what followed from it? Two of Eli’s sons proved sacrilegious and wicked priests. Two of David’s sons proved very bad members of the commonwealth, even plain traitors.

=== §. 9. #emph[Of the apostle’s order in laying down the duties of husbands and wives in the first place.]

There being three special degrees or orders in a family, #footnote[Why the duties of man and wife are delivered first.] (as we heard before) the apostle places husband and wife in the first rank, and declares their duties first, and that not without good reason. For

First, the husband and wife were the first couple that #footnote[1\. Man and wife were the first couple.] ever were in the world. Adam and Eve were joined in marriage, and made man and wife, before they had children or servants. So it happens for the most part even to this day in setting up or bringing together a family: the first couple is ordinarily a husband and a wife.

Secondly, most usually the husband and his wife are the #footnote[2\. Man and wife are commonly the chief in a family.] chief in a family, and all under them are single persons: they are the governors of all the rest in the house. Therefore it is most fitting that they should first know their duty and learn to practice it, so that they may be an example to all the rest. If they fail in their duty to one another, they give occasion to all the rest under them to be careless and negligent in theirs. Let a husband be churlish to his wife and despise her, and he gives an occasion to children and servants to despise her likewise and to be disobedient to her. Yes, he gives them occasion to be churlish and perverse to one another, especially to those under them. Let a wife be untrustworthy and unfaithful to her husband, let her filch and pilfer from him, and children and servants will soon take courage, or rather boldness, from her example to steal secretly what they can from their father and master. So their breach of #footnote[Bis peccat qui exemplo peccat. \[He sins twice who sins by example.\]] duty is a double fault: one in respect of the party whom they wrong and to whom they deny duty, the other in respect of those to whom they give occasion of sinning.

Know therefore, O husbands and wives, that you#super[+], above #footnote[Use.] all others in the family, are most bound to a conscientious performance of your duty. Your#super[+] condemnation will be greater if you fail in it. Look to it above the rest, and by your#super[+] example draw your children and servants (if you have any) to perform their duties. They will surely do this more readily when they see you#super[+] as guides going before them, and making conscience of your joint and separate duties.

=== §. 10. #emph[Of the apostle’s order in setting down the duties of inferiors in the first place.] #footnote[Wives’ particular duties are laid down first because they are inferiors.]

In handling the duties of the first couple named above, the apostle begins with wives, and lays down their particular duties in the first place. I take the reason for this order to be the #emph[inferiority] of the wife to her husband. I take it so all the more because I observe this to be his usual method and order: first to declare the duties of inferiors, and then those of superiors. For in handling the duties of #footnote[Eph 6:1.] children and parents, and of #footnote[Eph 6:5.] servants and masters, he begins with the inferiors, both in this and #footnote[Col 3:18, 20, 22.] in other epistles. Peter#footnote[1 Pet 3:1.] also observes this order. Yes, #footnote[Exod 20:12.] the law itself mentions the inferiors’ duty in the first place, and that expressly, only implying the superiors’ duty to follow as a just consequence, which is this: #emph[If the inferior must give honor, and by virtue of it perform such duties as belong to it, then the superior must carry himself as worthy of honor, and by virtue of it perform corresponding duties.]

#emph[Question.] Why should inferiors’ duties be more fully #footnote[Inferiors’ duties taught first, because they are most backward and reluctant to perform them.] expressed, and placed in the first rank?

#emph[Answer.] Surely because, for the most part, inferiors are most unwilling to undertake the duties of their place. Who is not more ready to rule than to be subject?

I do not deny that it is a far more difficult and hard matter to govern well than to obey well. For to rule and #footnote[Greater difficulty in ruling well than in obeying.] govern requires more knowledge, experience, wisdom, care, watchfulness, diligence and other such virtues than to obey and be subject. He who obeys has his rule laid before him, which is the will and command of his #footnote[Ducem velim qui à fronte pariter, & tergo sit oculatus. \[I would have a leader who has eyes both in front and behind.\] Timoth. apud Plutarch.] superior in things lawful and not against God’s will. But the superior who commands has to consider not only what is lawful, but also what is most fit, proper, suitable and in every way the best. Yes, he must also look ahead to the time to come, and observe, as far as he can, whether what is fit enough for the present may not be dangerous for the time to come, and in that respect unfit to be pressed. From this it follows that the #emph[superior in authority] may sin in commanding what the #emph[inferior in subjection] may do at his command without sin. Who can justly charge Joab with sin in numbering the people,#footnote[2 Sam 24:2, #emph[etc.]] when David urged him to do it by virtue of his authority?#footnote[See #emph[Treatise] 7, §. 37.] Yet David did sin in commanding it. Without any question Saul did sin in charging the people by an oath to eat no food on the day that they pursued their enemies (a time when they most needed to be refreshed with food, as Jonathan’s words imply). And yet the people did not #footnote[1 Sam 14:24. #emph[etc.]] sin in abstaining: witness what followed on Jonathan’s eating, though he did not know his father’s charge. Who does not see by this that it is a matter of much more difficulty to rule well than to obey? This is still further evident from God’s wise, disposing providence in ordering who should govern and who obey. Commonly the younger #footnote[#emph[Seniores & inter eos optimos, & prudentes & potentes oportet imperare. Iuniores vero parere.] \[The elders, and among them the best, the wise and the able, ought to rule; the younger ought to obey.\] #emph[Plat. de rep. lib] 3.] in age, the weaker in sex, the lower in condition, the more ignorant in understanding, and the like, are in places of subjection; but the elder, stronger, wealthier, wiser and similar persons are for the most part, or at least should be, in places of authority. “Woe to you, O land#footnote[Eccl 10:16.] whose king is a youth,” says Solomon. And Isaiah#footnote[Isa 3:4.] denounces it as a curse on Israel: “I will make mere lads their leaders, and children will rule over them.” And he complains #footnote[Isa 3:12.] that “women rule over them.”

Now to return to the point: though it is true that governors have the heaviest burden laid on their shoulders, yet inferiors who are under subjection think their own burden the heaviest. They are the most reluctant to bear it and the most willing to cast it off. For by nature there is in everyone much #footnote[Ambition is what makes inferiors reluctant to be subject.] pride and ambition. Like dust thrown in the eyes of their understanding, it puts out their sight, and so makes them desire superiority and authority over others, and makes them stubborn under the yoke of subjection. This is why in all ages, both by divine and by human laws, penalties and punishments of various kinds have been ordained to keep inferiors within the bounds of their duty. And yet (such is the pride of man’s heart) all this will not serve. What age, what place was there ever, which has #footnote[2\. Inferiors’ duties delivered first, to teach them how to win their governors’ favor.] not had just cause to complain of subjects’ rebellion, servants’ stubbornness, children’s disobedience and wives’ presumption? It is not without cause, therefore, that the apostle first declares the duties of inferiors.

Besides, the apostle would teach by this those who are under authority how to move those who are in authority over them to deal fairly and kindly with them, not harshly and cruelly: namely, by endeavoring to perform their own duty first. For what is it that provokes wrath, rage and fury in governors? What makes those who have authority deal roughly and rigorously? Is it not, for the most part, disobedience and stubbornness in those who are under government? Some in authority are so proud, so savage and inhumane that no honor done to them, no performance of duty, can satisfy and content them, but they will (like David’s#footnote[Ps 38:20.] enemies) repay goodness with evil. Even so, the best general direction that can be prescribed to inferiors, to provoke their governors to deal well with them, is that the inferiors themselves be careful and conscientious in doing their duty first. If their governors on earth are not moved by it at all, yet the highest Lord in heaven will graciously accept it.

Lastly, men must first learn to obey well before they #footnote[3\. By obeying, men learn to rule well. #emph[Parendo disces imperare. Arist. Polit. lib.] 3. #emph[c.] 3. #emph[Use.]] can rule well. For those who scorn to be subject to their governors while they are under authority are likely to prove intolerably insolent when they are in authority.

Learn, all you#super[+] who are under authority, how to win your governors’ favor: how to make your yoke easy and your burden light, and how to prevent many harms which might otherwise fall on you because of the power your superiors have over you. First do your#super[+] duty.

There are many weighty reasons to move governors #footnote[Reasons why governors ought to perform their duties first.] to be the first to begin doing their duty. For,

First, by virtue of their authority they bear God’s image; therefore in doing their duty they honor that image.

Secondly, because of their place they ought to go before those who are under them.

Thirdly, a faithful performance of their duty is a special means to keep their inferiors within the bounds of theirs.

Fourthly, their failing in duty sets an example: it causes others under them to fail in theirs, and so it is a double sin.

Fifthly, their reckoning will be the greater: for of those who have received more, more will be required.

It is therefore to be wished that superiors and inferiors would strive over who should begin first,#footnote[#emph[Vterque suum praeoccupet officium.] \[Let each be first to take up his own duty.\] #emph[Chrys. hom.] 26. #emph[in] 1 Cor 11.] and who should perform his own part best, and in this kind strive to excel, as runners in a race strive to outrun one another.

But if the question is raised who will begin, I advise inferiors not to hold out in this contest, but to consider that the apostle #footnote[Reasons why inferiors ought to perform their duties first.] urges them first, and that it is safest for them to begin. For in this contest inferiors are likely to fare the worst, because of the power which superiors have over them. And though it is more against our corrupt, proud and stubborn nature to be subject and obey, yet let us endeavor all the more to yield duty in this kind. For it is a special part of spiritual prudence to observe what our corrupt nature is most prone to, and where it most swells up, so that there we may strive most to beat it down. Nature is contrary to grace, and “the mind of the flesh is hostile to God.”#footnote[Rom 8:7.]

=== §. 11. #emph[Of the reasons why wives’ duties are taught first.]

#emph[Question.] Why, among other inferiors, are wives brought first into the school of Christ to learn their duty?

#emph[Answer.] Many good reasons may be given for the apostle’s order even in this point.

First, of all the other inferiors in a family, wives are by far the most excellent, and therefore they are to be placed in the first rank.

Secondly, wives were the first to whom subjection was commanded. Before there was child or servant in the world, it was said to the woman, “Your desire will be subject to your husband.” #footnote[Gen 3:16.]

Thirdly, wives are the fountain from which all the other degrees spring, and therefore they ought to be cleansed first.

Fourthly, this subjection is a good pattern for children and servants, and a great means to move them to be subject.

Fifthly, I may further add as a truth, which experience makes all too plain in every place, that among all the parties from whom the Holy Spirit requires subjection, wives for the most part are #footnote[See #emph[Treatise] 3, §. 4.] the most backward in yielding subjection to their husbands. But you#super[+] wives who fear God, be careful of your duty. Though it may seem somewhat contrary to #footnote[Exod 23:2.] the common course and practice of wives, yet “you shall not follow the crowd in wrongdoing.” Though it is harsh to corrupt nature, #footnote[#emph[Si propter Deum viro parueris, noli proponere quae ab eo fieri deceant, sed ea quibus te legislator fecit obnoxiam diligenter exequere.] \[If you obey your husband for God’s sake, do not set before you what it becomes him to do, but diligently carry out what the lawgiver has bound you to.\] #emph[Chrys. hom.] 26. #emph[in] 1 Cor 11.] yet beat down that corruption. Yes, though your#super[+] husbands are backward in their duties, yet be eager, and strive to go before them in yours. Remember what the Lord says (Matt 5:46, 47): “If you#super[+] love those who love you, what are you doing more than others?” Remember also what the apostle says (1 Tim 2:14): that the woman was first in the transgression, #footnote[Gen 3:16.] and first had her duty given to her, and that the woman was made for the man, and not man for the woman.#footnote[1 Cor 11:9.]

So you#super[+] will deserve that commendation of good wives: “Many have done noble things, but you surpass them all.”#footnote[Prov 31:29.]

Having so far handled the general instructions named above, I will go on to a more distinct explanation of the words, gather the observations which arise from them, and then declare in particular the separate duties which the three orders in a family owe to each other.

=== §. 12. #emph[Of wives’ subjection.]

