#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[The] #footnote[ὑποτάσσεσθε.] word by which the apostle has set out the duties of wives is in the middle voice. It may be translated passively, as #footnote[Subditae sint \[Let them be subject\]. Hieron. Vet. Trans. Erasm.] many have done, or #footnote[Subijcite vos \[Submit yourselves\]. Beza.] actively, as our English Bible does #emph[(submit yourselves),] and that most fitly: for there is a double subjection.

1\. A #emph[necessary] subjection, which is the subjection of #emph[order.]

2\. A #emph[voluntary] subjection, which is the subjection of #emph[duty.] The necessary subjection is that degree of inferiority in which God has placed all inferiors, and by which he has subjected them to their superiors, that is, set them in a lower rank. By virtue of it, though inferiors seek to exalt themselves above their superiors, yet they are subject to them: their ambition does not take away the order which God has established. A wife is in an inferior degree, however much she domineers over her husband.

The #emph[voluntary] subjection is that dutiful respect which inferiors carry toward those whom God has set over them, by which they show a willingness to yield to the order which God has established. Because God has placed them under their superiors, they will show, in all duty, the subjection which their place requires.

Because it is a duty that is required here, the #emph[voluntary subjection] must be meant here. And to express this, it is set down this way: #emph[submit yourselves.]

Though the same word is used here as in the previous verse, yet it is limited to a narrower compass, namely to #footnote[See §. 3.] #emph[the subjection of reverence.]

Here #emph[learn that voluntary subjection must be added to necessary] #footnote[1\. #emph[Observation.]] #emph[subjection:] that is, duty must be performed according to the order and degree in which God has set us. This is to make a virtue of necessity.

Under this phrase #emph[(submit yourselves)] all the duties which a wife owes to her husband are included, as I will #footnote[#emph[Treatise] 3, §. 2.] show more distinctly later.

=== §. 13. #emph[Of the persons to whom wives must be subject.]

In setting down the persons to whom wives owe subjection, the apostle notes a word of limitation#footnote[ἰδίοις.] (#emph[own]), and he does so to show that a wife ought to have only one husband. This is expressed more plainly in another place by the same phrase: “each woman” is to have “her own husband,” that #footnote[1 Cor 7:2 τὸν ἴδιον ἄνδρα. See §. 82, 83.] is, only one, her own. So

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[It is unlawful for a wife to have more than one husband at once.]]

A wife must submit herself only to that one #footnote[2\. #emph[Observation.]] husband of her own, and to no other man (as she is a wife and yields the duty of a wife). So the subjection of adulteresses is excluded here, and the duty required is that

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[A wife must yield a chaste, faithful, matrimonial subjection] #footnote[3\. #emph[Observation.]] #emph[to her husband.]]

Here, by the way, note the foolish #footnote[#emph[Adamiani ex Adam dicti, cuius imitantur in paradiso nuditatem. Nuptias auersantur. Nudi itaque mares foeminaeque conueniunt, etc.] \[The Adamians, named after Adam, whose nakedness in paradise they imitate. They reject marriage. And so men and women gather together naked, etc.\] #emph[August. de Haeres. distinct.] 31. #emph[Promiscuè inter se viri & foeminae, Veneri operam dabant.] \[Men and women gave themselves to lust promiscuously with one another.\] #emph[Philast.]] inference of Adamites, Familists and such licentious libertines. From the general words which the apostle uses #emph[(men] #footnote[οἱ ἄνδρες. αἱ γυναῖκες.] and #emph[women)] they infer that all women are as wives to all men, and that there is no need for any such close union of one man with one woman. This beastly opinion is contrary to the current of Scripture and to the ancient law of marriage (“the two will become one flesh”), and so also to this clause #emph[(their own husbands).] The apostle, in using those general words, followed the Greek idiom, which puts those two words #emph[(men, women)] for husbands and wives. So also do other languages, yes, and our English. The particular relation between the persons meant by those two words plainly shows how they are to be taken, and when they are to be limited to man and wife. To take away that ambiguity, our English has well translated them #emph[husband] and #emph[wife.]

To direct wives to their duty and stir them up to it, the apostle adds this clause #emph[(as to the Lord),] which is both a #emph[rule] and a #emph[reason] for wives’ subjection. It directs wives by noting the #emph[limit] of their obedience and its manner.

The limit is in this: that #emph[wives ought to obey their husbands in such a way] #footnote[4\. #emph[Observation.] See #emph[Treatise] 3, §. 51.] #emph[that at the same time they obey the Lord;] but no further. They must not be subject to their husbands in anything that cannot stand with their subjection to the Lord.

The #emph[manner] is in this: that #emph[wives ought to yield such a kind of subjection] #footnote[5\. #emph[Observation.] See #emph[Treatise] 3, §. 54.] #emph[to their husbands as may be approved by the Lord.]

So the apostle himself explains this phrase, in ch. 6, v. 5, 6.

It stirs wives up to submit themselves to their husbands by noting the place of a husband, which is to be in the Lord’s stead, bearing his image, and in that respect having a fellowship and partnership with the Lord. So

#emph[Wives, in subjecting themselves rightly to their husbands, are subject to the Lord.] And on the other side, #footnote[6\. #emph[Observation.]]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Wives, in refusing to be subject to their husbands, refuse to be subject to the Lord.] #footnote[7\. #emph[Observation.] See #emph[Treatise] 3, §. 70.]]
